## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about multi-key sorting, stability, and practical trade-offs in beginner-friendly tasks. It also aligns with real-world data processing where groupings matter.

## Key Concepts

- Sorting stability and its impact on multi-key ordering
- Two-pass stable sorting technique
- Time complexity: O(n log n) for mergesort
- Space complexity: O(n) auxiliary memory

## Code Example

```javascript
function stableMergeSortBy(arr, key) {
  if (arr.length <= 1) return arr
  const mid = Math.floor(arr.length / 2)
  const left = stableMergeSortBy(arr.slice(0, mid), key)
  const right = stableMergeSortBy(arr.slice(mid), key)
  const out = []
  let i = 0, j = 0
  while (i < left.length && j < right.length) {
    if (left[i][key] <= right[j][key]) out.push(left[i++])
    else out.push(right[j++])
  }
  return out.concat(left.slice(i)).concat(right.slice(j))
}

function sortRecords(arr) {
  const byTime = stableMergeSortBy(arr, 'time')
  return stableMergeSortBy(byTime, 'priority')
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you modify if you must sort in place and the library sort is not stable?
- If time values span a large range, how could you optimize space or use counting/radix techniques to reduce complexity?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests dynamic dictionary updates with a prefix-based query, combining trie augmentation and edge-case handling. 

## Key Concepts
- Trie with per-node passCount to track subtree content
- O(length of word) updates for insert/delete
- LCP query via prefix traversal until no valid child
- Edge cases: duplicates, deleting absent words, memory pruning

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Map();
    this.cnt = 0;
  }
}
class DynamicDict {
  constructor(){ this.root = new TrieNode(); this.hasWord=new Set(); }
  insert(w){ if(this.hasWord.has(w)) return; this.hasWord.add(w); let node=this.root; for(const ch of w){ if(!node.next.has(ch)) node.next.set(ch,new TrieNode()); node=node.next.get(ch); node.cnt++; } }
  delete(w){ if(!this.hasWord.has(w)) return; this.hasWord.delete(w); let node=this.root; for(const ch of w){ const child=node.next.get(ch); child.cnt--; if(child.cnt===0){ node.next.delete(ch); } node=child; } }
  lcpQuery(q){ let node=this.root, depth=0, res=0; for(const ch of q){ if(!node.next.has(ch)) break; node=node.next.get(ch); depth++; if(node.cnt>0) res=depth; } return res; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you support Unicode or a larger alphabet efficiently? 
- Compare memory trade-offs with an augmented BST of words versus the Trie approach.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP comprehension with a practical constraint (exact picks, non-adjacency) and both iterative and recursive styles.

## Key Concepts
